{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"fc98012095e543905f4f66ac6622c0155e4e171b306ad6393494b42fcfc180ab","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_a185333a2cc90be06faf01f00bab215e8b0f5339fed68f63b712daaf5bd60ab4","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a systematic theoretical and practical framework for modularized network morphing, advancing the field by moving from primitive layer-level operations to a general module-level graph transformation. The theoretical contribution, specifically the proof that any single-source, single-sink DAG module can be morphed from a convolutional layer, provides a significant conceptual upper bound for network evolution. Empirically, the method demonstrates substantial performance gains over ResNet baselines on CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet with minimal computational overhead, suggesting a viable pathway for efficient model improvement and architecture exploration.","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","paper_identity_sha256":"3fb2b8028c8cc8a0170d76a9b214907859cc5d539f6c17a2723e17ada69ae93d","prompt_identity_sha256":"7d377bf267b70054aa44a0d39ca66a479606149ddabe972a7482baea43a58f1a","raw_response_sha256":"51f88c34df97c24ee6cfe83c81cce7284c68eba3f9300ec934a14389da8fc3c5","request_identity_sha256":"832056e049051001a36a98c3f88830ddb3d31a81efba16dafc556e33902ebf40","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"d8314c239b93edd21f79e8a6af92616cb39270808df24fa4ef7b298ed0b8b379","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-004","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The work addresses a fundamental challenge in deep learning: the lack of backward compatibility in evolving neural network architectures. By formulating network morphism as a graph transformation problem and proving that any reasonable module can be morphed from a single convolutional layer, the paper provides a theoretical upper bound for this learning scheme. This generalization from primitive layer-level operations to a unified module-level framework has the potential to influence multiple research areas, including lifelong learning, model compression, and architecture search, by offering a rigorous method to update systems without re-learning from scratch.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-010","SRC-034"],"review_text":"The contribution is highly relevant to the domain of computer vision, specifically for modularized architectures like ResNet and GoogLeNet. The paper demonstrates that the proposed morphing operations can be directly applied to these state-of-the-art networks, addressing the practical need to update complex, stacked construction units. The experimental results on CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet show that the method effectively improves performance on standard benchmarks, validating its utility for researchers working on image classification and network evolution.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-007","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-036","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper reports substantial performance improvements over the ResNet baselines. On CIFAR10, the morphed 20-layer network achieves an error rate of 6.60%, which is lower than the 110-layer ResNet (6.61%) with significantly less computational cost. The morphed 56-layer network achieves 5.37%, outperforming ResNet-110 and ResNet-164. On CIFAR100 and ImageNet, consistent absolute and relative performance gains are observed (e.g., up to 2% absolute improvement on CIFAR100 and 1% on ImageNet) with only 1.1x to 1.2x computational overhead. These results indicate that the method offers a more efficient path to high performance compared to simply increasing network depth.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-007"],"review_text":"The paper highlights the potential for life-long learning systems that can evolve and update themselves without re-learning from scratch, which has implications for reducing computational resources and energy consumption in continuous learning scenarios. By enabling the inheritance of knowledge from well-trained networks, the method could facilitate more sustainable and efficient deployment of AI systems. However, the paper does not extensively discuss broader societal impacts, fairness, or specific deployment risks beyond the computational efficiency gains.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"746e2e470b11a75ee19074e93caebf0ad6fa06b9ca264daca6a14e8e76a67952","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","paper_ordinal":30,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"51f88c34df97c24ee6cfe83c81cce7284c68eba3f9300ec934a14389da8fc3c5","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"55e0326fcbbe328ae5f4a63265e48674df25ed2b4759a6b21dd977118969be3e","request_identity_sha256":"832056e049051001a36a98c3f88830ddb3d31a81efba16dafc556e33902ebf40","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":892,"prompt_tokens":20395,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":21287},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"832056e049051001a36a98c3f88830ddb3d31a81efba16dafc556e33902ebf40","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}